      "grammar-scanlon-1944"
    ]
  },
  "learningBlocks": [
    {
      "id": "l36-b1",
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "Poetry moves words, not grammar",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Hymns, Psalms and antiphons often separate words that belong together or omit elements recoverable from parallel lines. The case endings still carry the syntax.",
        "fr": "Hymnes, psaumes et antiennes séparent souvent des mots qui vont ensemble ou omettent des éléments récupérables grâce au parallélisme. Les désinences continuent de porter la syntaxe."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-plater-white-1926",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "La poésie déplace les mots, pas la grammaire"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l36-b2",
      "type": "hymn",
      "title": "Pange, lingua",
      "sourceId": "pange-lingua-l36",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "The hymn addresses the tongue directly. Pange is the command; lingua is vocative; Corporis mysterium is the object phrase despite the compressed poetic order.",
        "fr": "L'hymne s'adresse directement à la langue. Pange est l'ordre ; lingua est au vocatif ; Corporis mysterium est le groupe objet malgré l'ordre poétique condensé."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "pange-lingua-l36",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-plater-white-1926",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Pange, lingua"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l36-b3",
      "type": "psalm",
      "title": "Qui plantavit aurem, non audiet?",
      "sourceId": "vulg-ps93-9-l36",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "The Psalm uses a rhetorical question whose answer is obvious. Biblical poetry often teaches through balanced questions rather than explicit propositions.",
        "fr": "Le psaume emploie une question rhétorique dont la réponse est évidente. La poésie biblique enseigne souvent par questions équilibrées plutôt que par affirmations explicites."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "vulg-ps93-9-l36",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-plater-white-1926",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Qui plantavit aurem, non audiet?"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l36-b4",
      "type": "poetry",
      "title": "Recover the relationships",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "In A solis ortu usque ad occasum, the prepositions and cases create the full span even though the clause has no ordinary finite verb. Supply only what the syntax and parallel sense require.",
        "fr": "Dans A solis ortu usque ad occasum, les prépositions et les cas créent l'étendue complète bien que la proposition n'ait pas de verbe conjugué ordinaire. Ne suppléez que ce que syntaxe et parallélisme exigent."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-plater-white-1926",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Retrouver les relations grammaticales"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l36-b5",
      "type": "imagery",
      "title": "Concrete nouns become liturgical images",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "fire, sea, sun, stone, foot and tongue remain concrete nouns, yet Scripture and hymnody give them recurring spiritual or typological associations. Read literal syntax before interpreting imagery.",
        "fr": "feu, mer, soleil, pierre, pied et langue restent des noms concrets, mais l'Écriture et l'hymnographie leur donnent des associations spirituelles ou typologiques récurrentes. Lisez d'abord la syntaxe littérale."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-plater-white-1926",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "Les noms concrets deviennent des images liturgiques"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "l36-b6",
      "type": "independence",
      "title": "Assistance now recedes",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "At Stage 8, glosses should rescue genuinely unknown words, not replace parsing. Mark endings, recover omitted structure, and only then consult support.",
        "fr": "À l'étape 8, les gloses doivent seulement secourir les mots réellement inconnus, non remplacer l'analyse. Repérez les désinences, reconstruisez la structure omise, puis consultez l'aide."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-plater-white-1926",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ],
      "localization": {
        "fr": {
          "title": "L’assistance diminue désormais"
        }
      }
    }
  ],
